> Introduction
> 
> In recent years, the world has witnessed a rapid increase in the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events. From devastating hurricanes and floods to prolonged droughts and wildfires, these events have wreaked havoc across the globe, affecting millions of lives and livelihoods. Such localised disasters can have far-reaching macroeconomic consequences, undermining national economic growth and upending government budgets. Climate impacts can ripple through national economies, exacerbating poverty and inequality, while stalling development efforts.
> 
> Climate change doesn’t just threaten current economic stability; it poses measurable risks to government finances by increasing public expenditures for disaster relief and recovery efforts, while simultaneously reducing revenue streams and potentially lowering sovereign credit ratings. This precarious fiscal situation highlights the urgent need for robust measures to mitigate these growing threats.
> 
> Fortunately, strategic investments in adaptation and resilience offer significant opportunities for managing these risks. By implementing adaptive measures, countries can significantly reduce the potential damages associated with climate change. Several National Adaptation Plans (NAPs) already illustrate innovative solutions being employed across different regions to bolster resilience. From infrastructure modifications to ecosystem-based approaches, these examples underscore that proactive adaptation is both feasible and effective.
> 
> Studies consistently demonstrate high rates of return on investments in adaptation, particularly in terms of risk reduction. Beyond reducing vulnerabilities, these investments frequently yield substantial co-benefits, such as support for economic development and provision of social and environmental advantages—a concept often termed the “triple dividend” of adaptation.
> 
> What We Do in This Report
> 
> This report aims to conduct a global review and synthesis of existing evidence to assess the economic costs of inaction and the benefits of adaptation investments. By focusing on macroeconomic and fiscal outcomes at the national level, we highlight the critical importance of proactive, public measures to address climate impacts.
> 
> Our results are all based on the existing evidence base in the academic and grey literature. While the available evidence on macroeconomic risks has evolved rapidly in recent years, our knowledge of the socioeconomic consequences of climate change and the potential for adaptation remains highly uncertain, sorely incomplete, and noticeably biased toward certain countries.
> 
> Despite this, the evidence on climate impacts and adaptation is unequivocal and compelling, highlighting the urgent need for concrete responses. Socioeconomic impacts of climate change will be highly disruptive across the globe, from the individual to the macroeconomic levels. Existing adaptation actions offer large benefits across a wide range of sectors. The presence of uncertainty in this context motivates strong, precautionary action, to avoid the worst outcomes.
> 
> Recognizing the need for actionable insights, this report targets Ministries of Finance, which oversee public financing exceeding $30 trillion per annum globally, and other economic decision-makers. Our findings emphasize the potential advantages of integrating adaptation strategies into fiscal planning, and how these investments can safeguard public finances while simultaneously fulfilling national development objectives. 
> 
> Main results
> 
> As the global climate crisis intensifies, the need for investment in adaptation strategies has become more pressing than ever. Severe losses lie ahead; without stronger action, some nations in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia could face GDP losses in 2050 exceeding 20% due to temperature shocks alone, while total welfare losses are expected to be far higher. Alarmingly, many least developed countries are already experiencing the negative impacts of climate change, with estimates suggesting they could be 10% poorer today as a result of current warming.
> 
> Labour productivity loss is projected to exceed 5% in regions like Africa and Asia, while TFP growth could be reduced by up to 0.3 percentage points per year, suggesting potential long-term economic stagnation. These macroeconomic changes are projected to have fiscal consequences that grow through mid-century. Climate change is expected to threaten sovereign credit ratings across the globe, especially in high-income countries. 
> 
> However, there is a ray of hope in the form of smart investment in adaptation. Adaptation actions deliver a median economic benefit-cost ratio of at least 4 to 1, and the median internal rate of return is an impressive 25%. This makes adaptation not just a necessary response but one of the best economic investments available, capable of halving the projected impacts of climate change on economies.
> 
> Proactive action on the part of governments, including MoF, can both reduce these risks and pave the way to concessionary funding. As a national average, adaptation financing that supports locally-targeted adaptation can reduce risks by 38 - 72% (World Bank 2024). The rise in borrowing costs can be reduced or reversed through investment in resilience with every 1% change in resilience reducing the change in borrowing costs by 0.75% in developing countries (IMF 2020).
> 
> Overall, these findings underscore the critical role of proactive adaptation strategies in mitigating both immediate and long-term economic and welfare losses, highlighting the importance of investing in resilience to safeguard vulnerable economies against the escalating risks of climate change.
